.TH TLS 2
.SH NAME
tls \- TLS server sessions from BearSSL, with record primitives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "tls.m";
tls := load TLS TLS->PATH;

(h, err) := tls->accept(fd, cert, key);
(h, err) = tls->acceptpre(fd, cert, key, prefix);
n := tls->read(h, buf);
n = tls->write(h, buf);
tls->close(h);

(cert, key, err) := tls->certgen(cn, notbefore, notafter);
ok := tls->ecverify(pub, hash, sig);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tls
is a builtin module compiled into the Purgatorio kernel.  It wraps an
already-connected file descriptor in a server-side TLS 1.2 session
using the BearSSL sources in
.BR /libbearssl .
Only TLS 1.2 is supported, with the suite
.BR ECDHE_ECDSA_AES128_GCM_SHA256 ;
the certificate must be an ECDSA P-256 leaf and
.I key
is its 32-byte private scalar.
.PP
.B accept
returns a small integer handle, or \-1 with an error string.  The
handshake is performed lazily by the first
.B read
or
.BR write .
The caller owns
.I fd
and must close it after
.BR close .
.PP
.B acceptpre
is
.B accept
with a prefix: the bytes in
.I prefix
were already read from
.I fd
(for example by peeking at a ClientHello to choose a protocol
version) and are replayed to BearSSL before it reads the file again.
Pass a zero-length array to mean none.
.PP
.B certgen
mints a self-signed ECDSA P-256 certificate.  The times are UTCTime
strings,
.IB YYMMDDhhmmssZ .
It returns the DER certificate and the private scalar; the caller
persists both.  Gemini uses trust-on-first-use, so no CA is involved.
.PP
The remaining functions expose the pieces a TLS 1.3 handshake needs.
.B gcmseal
encrypts
.I plain
with AES-128-GCM, returning ciphertext followed by the 16-byte tag;
.B gcmopen
returns the plaintext, or nil if the tag does not verify.  Both take a
12-byte nonce and treat
.I ad
as additional authenticated data.
.B sign
returns an ASN.1 DER ECDSA P-256/SHA-256 signature over
.I msg
using the same 32-byte scalar as a session.
.B ecverify
checks such a signature over a 32-byte hash against a 65-byte
uncompressed P-256 point (04||X||Y), returning 1 if it is valid.
.SH FILES
.TF /libbearssl
.TP
.B /libbearssl
vendored BearSSL sources, configuration and build script
.PP
The same implementation is compiled into the hosted emulator, so
.B tls
is available to Dis programs under
.B emu
as well as in the native kernel.  There it is built by the host C
compiler; the kernel build preprocesses the sources for 8c.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/pc/tls.c
.br
.B /module/tls.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gemserve (8),
.IR mono (2)
.SH BUGS
TLS 1.3 is not implemented.  The certificate path is ECDSA P-256 only;
there is no client-certificate verification, no session resumption, and
no renegotiation.
